1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,280
I'm just going to do one that I like better.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:05,880
It's not the only way.

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:06,880
There's another way.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:07,880
All right.

5
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:13,420
So, the first one, so if this is standard market, QP,

6
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:15,520
we've got supply, we've got demand.

7
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,420
And then if there's a positive externality,

8
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:20,820
one way we can diagram this is to add a social benefits

9
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:24,460
curve on top of the regular supply and demand graph.

10
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,860
And the social benefits curve includes all the products,

11
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:30,100
all the products, all the benefits, includes the bank

12
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:31,100
curve.

13
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,700
So, that new, so customers are part of society, right?

14
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,240
But it adds on this additional value from the bystanders.

15
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:40,780
Additional value from the bystanders.

16
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:43,580
So, social benefits with positive externalities,

17
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:49,460
of course, exceeds the private benefits.

18
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:52,620
And then similarly over here, if you have negative externalities,

19
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:56,260
then we've got supply, demand.

20
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,400
And now, with negative externalities,

21
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,860
we draw social benefits below the demand curve.

22
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:02,860
Because for every unit that's getting produced,

23
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:06,000
there's some bystanders who are choking or gagging

24
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,380
or looking in horror or disgust at what you're doing.

25
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:12,100
Or disappointment.

26
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:13,240
Percurbation.

27
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:16,620
You may hear some very mild disapproval.

28
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:19,180
But anyway, something negative, something negative,

29
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:21,180
where they have some willingness to pay.

30
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:24,460
Well, then why don't they pay if they're willing to pay?

31
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:26,340
Because of the non-excludability.

32
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:27,740
If one of these people here says,

33
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:31,660
hey, I'll go and pay you guys to produce less.

34
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:33,940
Then who gets the benefit?

35
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:36,380
All the people who are suffering get the benefit, not just

36
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:37,260
the payer.

37
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:40,260
So, the marginal gain for the payer is essentially nothing.

38
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:46,260
Now, one thing that you might have

39
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:47,860
noticed when you listen to economists

40
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,900
talk about the real world, is that they

41
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:52,980
seem to deviate from the textbook.

42
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:54,980
Because in the textbook, positive externalities

43
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:57,220
and negative externalities aren't the same thing

44
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:59,540
as public goods and public bads.

45
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:02,060
And in fact, there isn't any clear connection.

46
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,700
There isn't a clear, simple way to translate from one

47
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:05,820
to another.

48
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:08,620
But when economists actually talk about the real world,

49
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:10,460
they often will talk about, they often

50
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,460
will interchangeably say positive externalities

51
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:16,260
and public goods very, very easily.

52
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:18,940
And negative externalities and public bads very easily.

53
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:20,620
So people sometimes say, well, education

54
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:23,220
is positive externalities, so it's public good.

55
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:26,380
And say, well, the textbook said those were different things,

56
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,980
and kind of related, but not related in any simple way.

57
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:33,820
So, there's two things that we could do.

58
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:35,940
One thing is we could just reform our language,

59
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,620
so that we always use public good and public bads purely

60
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:40,220
in the textbook definition.

61
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:42,300
And then otherwise, we say positive externalities

62
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:43,900
and negative externalities.

63
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:45,540
So that's one option.

64
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:49,180
But another option is to pay more careful attention

65
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:51,740
to how would economists do in practice.

66
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:54,700
So when do economists say positive externalities

67
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:57,220
versus public goods in practice?

68
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:00,260
So I have a pet theory of this, of how economists

69
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:02,580
use the word in practice.

70
00:03:02,580 --> 00:03:04,340
We use both words in practice.

71
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:05,780
And now I will share with you.

72
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,980
You may decide for yourselves whether this accurately

73
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:12,260
describes usage of the terms.

74
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:16,420
This is what I think is going on.

75
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:19,540
So when do economists actually say public goods rather

76
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:21,500
than positive externalities?

77
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,660
So I say that economists say public goods when

78
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:27,220
it's an extreme positive externality.

79
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:30,060
So I say that when economists switch over

80
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:32,580
from saying positive externalities to public goods

81
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:35,620
when they think the social benefits are substantial,

82
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,900
but private demand is basically zero or negligible.

83
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:43,900
So this is not from any textbook I know of.

84
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:46,460
But if you just look at the way that economists use the word,

85
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:48,460
it seems to me that they talk about public goods

86
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:50,980
in cases of extreme positive externalities,

87
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:54,980
where the market equilibrium would be roughly zero.

88
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:58,380
And yet there is some big social benefit to existing.

89
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:01,460
And then similarly, I have an analogous claim

90
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:04,020
for public bads.

91
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:05,820
In practice, economists treat public bads

92
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:08,900
as being very extreme negative externalities,

93
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:11,940
so that these are goods where the social benefits are way

94
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:12,700
down there.

95
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:14,540
It would pretty much be better if the thing

96
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:19,300
didn't exist at all, but where demand is up there.

97
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:21,260
Now, what would be a difference to you?

98
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:22,660
What would be an example of this?

99
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,980
Well, so negative externalities, in a classic example,

100
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:27,340
that would be something like air pollution,

101
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:30,580
where there is a problem, and yet the products that

102
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:33,740
are making the air pollution are very useful.

103
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:34,580
So think about this.

104
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:37,820
Suppose that you could go and wave a wand right now

105
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:39,940
that would prevent all use of an internal combustion

106
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:41,820
engine on Earth.

107
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:44,260
This wouldn't give us any new way of getting energy,

108
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,220
at least at first.

109
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:49,460
It would just stop all internal combustion from working.

110
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:52,660
In fact, it would just stop all fossil fuels from working.

111
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:55,340
So what would likely happen over the next few months

112
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:56,380
after you wave that wand?

113
00:04:56,380 --> 00:04:56,900
What's that?

114
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,060
You've done terrible things.

115
00:04:58,060 --> 00:04:59,580
You guys are just great as monsters.

116
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:00,300
Yes, obviously.

117
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:00,980
Well, I bet.

118
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:02,980
So it seems very reasonable that most of mankind

119
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:06,660
would simply die, because our food production

120
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:08,180
uses fossil fuels.

121
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:10,560
Our transportation of the food, because remember, the food

122
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:11,820
is grown nearby.

123
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:13,860
So this would destroy both the production

124
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:16,340
and the transportation of food.

125
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:18,300
Now again, of course, if there were

126
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:20,020
time to do something different, maybe it

127
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:22,020
would be a different story.

128
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:23,940
So if you could wave a wand and simply

129
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:25,860
prevent anyone from using fossil fuels,

130
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:27,780
this would be a terrible thing to do.

131
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:29,220
Now, suppose you could wave a wand

132
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:32,540
and prevent anybody from murdering anybody else.

133
00:05:32,540 --> 00:05:35,460
How about that?

134
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:38,860
Well, I'd say murder is much more like a public bad,

135
00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:40,620
where you stop all murder from happening.

136
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:43,500
It's like, well, what if we really need to murder a person?

137
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:44,820
What if there's enormous benefits

138
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:46,940
from murdering somebody?

139
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:48,860
Really, the only good examples I know

140
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:51,100
of big benefits from murdering people

141
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:53,580
are to stop people from murdering people.

142
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:55,780
So if there's someone who's going

143
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:57,340
to go and detonate a nuclear weapon,

144
00:05:57,340 --> 00:05:59,220
then let's kill that guy to stop him murdering people.

145
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:01,260
If you already waved the wand that stops murder,

146
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:04,340
then we no longer need to go and murder people to stop murder,

147
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:06,020
because it's already stopped.

148
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:09,780
So other examples where there's really any big social benefit

149
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:11,580
to murdering somebody?

150
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:12,160
I don't know.

151
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:12,820
Maybe it's out there.

152
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:13,460
I don't think it much.

153
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:13,980
What's that?

154
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:15,500
It's almost a find out of existence,

155
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,460
because we find out that murder is understood.

156
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,740
Yeah.

157
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:21,460
But you could say, well, rudeness is a terrible problem.

158
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:23,740
And if we couldn't execute people for rudeness,

159
00:06:23,740 --> 00:06:25,620
then society would cease to exist.

160
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:29,060
What about communists, maybe, who aren't intentionally

161
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:30,180
trying to murder people?

162
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:30,680
Yes.

163
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:31,740
Well, you know.

164
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:35,580
So I guess it builds into the definition of murder is intent.

165
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:37,020
Or actually reckless indifference.

166
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:39,060
So I say the communists are guilty.

167
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:41,940
There are others.

168
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:45,100
But now, think about just a milder case.

169
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:49,940
How about waving a wand to prevent all car theft?

170
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:51,140
I mean, there he's all right.

171
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:53,140
Well, maybe someone would need to steal a car

172
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:55,940
because they're being chased by a murderer.

173
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:57,340
Something like that.

174
00:06:57,340 --> 00:07:00,380
But still, it seems like if you could just wave a wand

175
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:02,380
and stop all that from happening,

176
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:05,500
that would probably be quite great.

177
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:08,460
And even if there's a few optimal car

178
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:12,140
thefts that don't happen as a result of it still, big game.

179
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:13,860
So those are cases where they seem more

180
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:15,540
like the extreme ones.

